  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/brown-v-board-of-education/misconception",
   "@type": "uso:Misconception",
   "uso:fact": "The Topeka NAACP deliberately organized a class action with 13 parents on behalf of 20 children; Oliver Brown was recruited to join and named because he was the only man in the group, and the Supreme Court decided Kansas's case together with cases from South Carolina, Virginia, Delaware and the District of Columbia involving nearly 200 people.",
   "uso:myth": "The case began when Oliver Brown alone sued after his daughter Linda was turned away from a nearby white school and forced to walk far to a Black school.",
   "uso:why": "Reducing Brown to one family's story hides the years of organized legal strategy and the many students, parents, teachers and lawyers, in the South and beyond it, who took risks to bring the cases."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/brown-v-board-of-education/review/1",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why did the NAACP's lawyers value the Kansas case even though Topeka's Black and white schools were roughly comparable in buildings and resources?",
   "uso:answer": "Because the facilities were substantially comparable, the Kansas case forced the Court to decide whether segregation itself was unconstitutional rather than merely ordering states to equalize unequal schools, and it showed that school segregation was a national issue and not only a Southern one.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:brown-v-board-of-education"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/brown-v-board-of-education/review/2",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why was the District of Columbia case, Bolling v. Sharpe, decided separately from the four state cases?",
   "uso:answer": "The Fourteenth Amendment's Equal Protection Clause restrains the states, not the federal government, so segregation in the federal capital had to be challenged under the Fifth Amendment instead.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:brown-v-board-of-education"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/brown-v-board-of-education/review/3",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How did the Court's approach to remedies allow segregation to continue for years after 1954?",
   "uso:answer": "The 1954 ruling did not say how or when schools must desegregate; Brown II in 1955 left it to federal district courts to proceed \"with all deliberate speed,\" and opponents used that vague standard to delay, admit only token numbers of Black students, pass new segregation laws, or, as in Prince Edward County, close public schools from 1959 to 1964.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:brown-v-board-of-education"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/brown-v-board-of-education/timeline/1",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Students led by 16-year-old Barbara Rose Johns walk out of the overcrowded Robert Russa Moton High School in Farmville, Virginia.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1951-04-23"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/brown-v-board-of-education/timeline/10",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Attorneys ask the federal court to reopen the Topeka case to test whether segregation's effects remained (Brown III).",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1979"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/brown-v-board-of-education/timeline/2",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "NAACP lawyers file Davis v. County School Board of Prince Edward County, later joined to Brown.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1951-05-23"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/brown-v-board-of-education/timeline/3",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Supreme Court hears the first arguments in the consolidated school cases.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1952-12-09"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/brown-v-board-of-education/timeline/4",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Reargument begins on questions the Court posed, including the history of the Fourteenth Amendment and possible remedies.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1953-12-07"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/brown-v-board-of-education/timeline/5",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Court rules unanimously that segregated public schools violate the Equal Protection Clause; Bolling v. Sharpe is decided the same day.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1954-05-17"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/brown-v-board-of-education/timeline/6",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "In Brown II the Court orders desegregation to proceed with all deliberate speed under federal district courts.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1955-05-31"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/brown-v-board-of-education/timeline/7",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Southern Manifesto denouncing the decision is introduced in Congress.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1956-03-12"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/brown-v-board-of-education/timeline/8",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Prince Edward County, Virginia, cuts off public school funding rather than desegregate.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1959-06-26"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/brown-v-board-of-education/timeline/9",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Prince Edward County's public schools reopen after the Supreme Court's Griffin decision.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1964-09-08"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/bureau-of-ocean-energy-management/misconception",
   "@type": "uso:Misconception",
   "uso:fact": "The breakup was carried out administratively, by the Interior Secretary's orders of 2010. The House passed a bill to restructure the agency in July 2010, but Congress did not enact the split into law; the 2026 merger into the Marine Minerals Administration was likewise made by secretarial order.",
   "uso:myth": "After the Deepwater Horizon spill, Congress passed a law abolishing the Minerals Management Service and creating BOEM.",
   "uso:why": "Other large post-crisis reorganizations, such as the creation of the Department of Homeland Security, were done by statute, and news coverage often described the 2010–2011 changes as a sweeping reform without noting how they were made."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/bureau-of-ocean-energy-management/review/1",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why did the Interior Department split the Minerals Management Service into separate bodies after 2010?",
   "uso:answer": "One agency had been both selling offshore leases and collecting about $13 billion a year in revenue while also policing drilling safety; after the Deepwater Horizon blowout and earlier inspector-general findings of ethical lapses and conflicts of interest, Secretary Salazar's order separated leasing and planning (BOEM), safety enforcement (BSEE) and revenue collection (ONRR) so that their missions would not pull against one another.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:bureau-of-ocean-energy-management"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/bureau-of-ocean-energy-management/review/2",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How does BOEM's job differ from that of the Bureau of Safety and Environmental Enforcement?",
   "uso:answer": "BOEM decides where and when offshore resources are offered and manages them — the five-year oil and gas leasing program, renewable-energy leases, sand and gravel for coastal restoration, resource evaluation and environmental studies — while BSEE permits and inspects operations and enforces safety and environmental rules.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:bureau-of-ocean-energy-management"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/bureau-of-ocean-energy-management/review/3",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why is the 2026 merger into the Marine Minerals Administration disputed?",
   "uso:answer": "Interior and industry supporters say two overlapping bureaus administering one law cause delays and that a single agency can coordinate better without weakening safety standards; critics, including environmental groups and congressional Democrats, argue that recombining leasing and safety oversight removes the separation created after Deepwater Horizon.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:bureau-of-ocean-energy-management"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/bureau-of-ocean-energy-management/timeline/1",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Congress passes the Outer Continental Shelf Lands Act, affirming federal jurisdiction over offshore mineral resources and putting Interior in charge of leasing.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1953"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/bureau-of-ocean-energy-management/timeline/10",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Interior Department announces plans to merge BOEM and BSEE into a Marine Minerals Administration.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "2026-04-03"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/bureau-of-ocean-energy-management/timeline/11",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Secretary's Order 3451 establishes the Marine Minerals Administration and vests BOEM's functions in it, with a phased transition.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "2026-07-10"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/bureau-of-ocean-energy-management/timeline/2",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Amendments to the act strengthen environmental safeguards and coastal-state involvement and reinforce the five-year leasing program.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1978"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/bureau-of-ocean-energy-management/timeline/3",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Secretarial Order 3071 establishes the Minerals Management Service, which later combines offshore leasing, safety oversight and royalty collection.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1982-01-19"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/bureau-of-ocean-energy-management/timeline/4",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Energy Policy Act extends offshore authority to renewable energy such as wind, wave and ocean-current power.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "2005"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/bureau-of-ocean-energy-management/timeline/5",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Deepwater Horizon blowout in the Gulf of Mexico kills 11 workers.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "2010-04-20"
   }
  },
  {
