  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/cherokee-nation-v-georgia/misconception",
   "@type": "uso:Misconception",
   "uso:fact": "The Court never reached the merits; it dismissed the suit because it lacked jurisdiction, while describing the Cherokee Nation as a distinct political society capable of governing itself. A year later Worcester v. Georgia held Georgia's laws void inside the Nation.",
   "uso:myth": "In Cherokee Nation v. Georgia the Supreme Court upheld Georgia's laws and ruled that the Cherokee Nation had no sovereignty.",
   "uso:why": "Because the Cherokees lost the case and were later forcibly removed, the jurisdictional dismissal is easily remembered as a ruling on Georgia's power or a denial of Cherokee nationhood."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/cherokee-nation-v-georgia/review/1",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why did the Supreme Court refuse to hear the Cherokee Nation's suit against Georgia?",
   "uso:answer": "The Constitution lets the Court hear suits between a state and foreign states, and the majority held that the Cherokee Nation, though a distinct political society, was not a foreign state but a domestic dependent nation, partly because the commerce clause lists foreign nations, states and Indian tribes as separate categories; without jurisdiction it had to dismiss the case.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:cherokee-nation-v-georgia"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/cherokee-nation-v-georgia/review/2",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How did the 1831 dismissal help set up Worcester v. Georgia?",
   "uso:answer": "Marshall suggested the land question might be decided in a proper case with proper parties, and when Georgia convicted missionaries such as Samuel Worcester under its license law, their appeal from a state court gave the Supreme Court jurisdiction to rule that Georgia's laws had no force in the Cherokee Nation.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:cherokee-nation-v-georgia"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/cherokee-nation-v-georgia/review/3",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why is the case's guardian-and-ward language still important in federal Indian law?",
   "uso:answer": "Marshall's description of the tribes' relation to the United States as resembling that of a ward to its guardian became a root of the federal trust responsibility, the duty the federal government owes tribes to protect their remaining lands and provide services.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:cherokee-nation-v-georgia"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/cherokee-nation-v-georgia/timeline/1",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Cherokee Nation adopts a written constitution modeled on the U.S. Constitution.",
