  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/desegregation-of-little-rock-central-high-school/misconception",
   "@type": "uso:Misconception",
   "uso:fact": "Troops stayed all year while the Nine faced daily harassment and one of them was expelled; in September 1958 Faubus closed all four Little Rock high schools under a new state law, voters backed closure three to one, and the schools did not reopen until August 1959.",
   "uso:myth": "Once federal troops escorted the Little Rock Nine into Central High in September 1957, the school and the city's schools were desegregated and the crisis was over.",
   "uso:why": "The famous images of paratroopers escorting the students compress a multi-year struggle into one moment of victory and hide how long and how deliberately segregationists resisted after the troops arrived."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/desegregation-of-little-rock-central-high-school/review/1",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why did President Eisenhower send federal troops to Little Rock even though he was reluctant to act as a civil rights champion?",
   "uso:answer": "He framed the deployment as enforcing the orders of federal courts against mob obstruction, arguing that if the executive did not uphold court rulings the rule of law would collapse; he also worried that the crisis was damaging America's standing abroad during the Cold War.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:desegregation-of-little-rock-central-high-school"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/desegregation-of-little-rock-central-high-school/review/2",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How did the Little Rock crisis lead to Cooper v. Aaron, and what did the Court decide?",
   "uso:answer": "After a year of turmoil the school board sought to suspend its desegregation plan; in September 1958 the Supreme Court unanimously refused, holding under the Supremacy Clause that state officials cannot nullify federal court orders based on Brown or war against the Constitution they swore to support.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:desegregation-of-little-rock-central-high-school"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/desegregation-of-little-rock-central-high-school/review/3",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why do historians disagree about Governor Orval Faubus's motives?",
   "uso:answer": "Before 1957 he was seen as a racial moderate who had hired Black state employees and supported Black colleges, so his biographer argues his stand was a political calculation to win re-election in 1958, while some observers insisted catering to segregationists was out of character for him; many voters rewarded him for appearing to stand up to the federal government.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:desegregation-of-little-rock-central-high-school"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/desegregation-of-little-rock-central-high-school/timeline/1",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Supreme Court rules in Brown v. Board of Education that segregated public schools are unconstitutional.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1954-05-17"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/desegregation-of-little-rock-central-high-school/timeline/10",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Escorted by federal troops, the Little Rock Nine attend their first full day of classes.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1957-09-25"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/desegregation-of-little-rock-central-high-school/timeline/11",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The 101st Airborne returns to Fort Campbell, leaving federalized Guard units in charge for the rest of the year.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYearMonth",
    "@value": "1957-11"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/desegregation-of-little-rock-central-high-school/timeline/12",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Minnijean Brown is expelled after retaliating against harassment.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYearMonth",
    "@value": "1958-02"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/desegregation-of-little-rock-central-high-school/timeline/13",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Ernest Green becomes the first Black student to graduate from Central High.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYearMonth",
    "@value": "1958-05"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/desegregation-of-little-rock-central-high-school/timeline/2",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Little Rock School Board adopts Superintendent Virgil Blossom's gradual plan, beginning with token desegregation of Central High in 1957.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1955-05-24"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/desegregation-of-little-rock-central-high-school/timeline/3",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Federal judge Ronald Davies voids a state court injunction against integration and orders the district to proceed.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1957-08-28"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/desegregation-of-little-rock-central-high-school/timeline/4",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Governor Orval Faubus announces on television that he has called out the Arkansas National Guard.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1957-09-02"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/desegregation-of-little-rock-central-high-school/timeline/5",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Guardsmen turn the Black students away; fifteen-year-old Elizabeth Eckford walks alone past a jeering crowd.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1957-09-04"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/desegregation-of-little-rock-central-high-school/timeline/6",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Faubus and Eisenhower meet at Newport, Rhode Island, without reaching agreement.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1957-09-14"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/desegregation-of-little-rock-central-high-school/timeline/7",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Judge Davies orders Faubus and the Guard to stop interfering, and Faubus withdraws the troops.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1957-09-20"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/desegregation-of-little-rock-central-high-school/timeline/8",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The students enter Central, but a mob of more than 1,000 forms outside and they are taken out by lunchtime; Eisenhower issues Proclamation 3204.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1957-09-23"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/desegregation-of-little-rock-central-high-school/timeline/9",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Eisenhower signs Executive Order 10730, federalizing the Arkansas Guard and sending in the 101st Airborne Division.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1957-09-24"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/dred-scott-v-sandford/misconception",
   "@type": "uso:Misconception",
   "uso:fact": "The first was Marbury v. Madison in 1803; Dred Scott was the first time since then that the Court invalidated a federal statute, the Missouri Compromise's restriction on slavery.",
   "uso:myth": "Dred Scott v. Sandford was the first time the Supreme Court struck down an act of Congress.",
   "uso:why": "Remembering it as the second use of judicial review against Congress, after a 54-year gap, shows how unusual and consequential the Court's choice to rule on territorial slavery was."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/dred-scott-v-sandford/review/1",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why did Dred Scott's case end up in federal court after he lost in the Missouri Supreme Court?",
   "uso:answer": "Scott sued John F. A. Sanford, Irene Emerson's brother and the executor of John Emerson's estate, who was a citizen of New York; because the parties lived in different states, Scott could sue under the federal courts' diversity jurisdiction, which raised the question of whether he was a citizen at all.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:dred-scott-v-sandford"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/dred-scott-v-sandford/review/2",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How did the Court's ruling on citizenship let it avoid deciding Scott's case on the merits, and why did it go further anyway?",
   "uso:answer": "Holding that Scott was not a citizen meant the federal courts lacked jurisdiction and the case had to be dismissed; but after re-argument the majority also ruled that Congress could not ban slavery in the territories, partly because dissenting justices planned to argue that Congress had that power.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:dred-scott-v-sandford"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/dred-scott-v-sandford/review/3",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How did the decision deepen the sectional crisis before the Civil War?",
   "uso:answer": "It made the Republican goal of keeping slavery out of the territories unconstitutional, which Republicans saw as proof of the Slave Power's reach, and it undercut Stephen Douglas's popular sovereignty, prompting his Freeport Doctrine, which alienated Southern Democrats and helped split the Democratic Party.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:dred-scott-v-sandford"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/dred-scott-v-sandford/review/4",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Which constitutional change reversed the decision's ruling on citizenship?",
   "uso:answer": "The Fourteenth Amendment, ratified in 1868, whose first sentence made all persons born or naturalized in the United States citizens, following the Civil Rights Act of 1866.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:dred-scott-v-sandford"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/dred-scott-v-sandford/timeline/1",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Dr. John Emerson, an army surgeon, takes the enslaved Dred Scott to Fort Armstrong in the free state of Illinois.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1833-12-01"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/dred-scott-v-sandford/timeline/10",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Court rules 7 to 2 against Scott, with Chief Justice Roger B. Taney delivering the opinion of the Court.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1857-03-06"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/dred-scott-v-sandford/timeline/11",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Taylor Blow emancipates Dred and Harriet Scott.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1857-05-26"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/dred-scott-v-sandford/timeline/12",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Dred Scott dies of tuberculosis in St. Louis.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1858-09-17"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/dred-scott-v-sandford/timeline/13",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Congress bans slavery in all western territories, undoing the decision's holding on territorial slavery.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYearMonth",
    "@value": "1862-06"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/dred-scott-v-sandford/timeline/14",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Fourteenth Amendment's citizenship clause makes all persons born in the United States citizens, overturning the decision's citizenship holding.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1868"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/dred-scott-v-sandford/timeline/2",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Emerson is transferred to Fort Snelling in the Wisconsin Territory, where the Missouri Compromise barred slavery; there Scott marries Harriet Robinson.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1836-05-04"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/dred-scott-v-sandford/timeline/3",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Dred and Harriet Scott file separate freedom suits against Irene Emerson in the St. Louis Circuit Court.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1846-04-06"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/dred-scott-v-sandford/timeline/4",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The first trial ends against the Scotts on a technicality, and their lawyer wins a new trial.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1847-06-30"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/dred-scott-v-sandford/timeline/5",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "A St. Louis jury finds the Scotts free.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1850-01-12"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/dred-scott-v-sandford/timeline/6",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Missouri Supreme Court reverses, rejecting the principle of once free, always free.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1852-03-22"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/dred-scott-v-sandford/timeline/7",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "In the federal suit against John F. A. Sanford, Judge Robert W. Wells rules that Missouri law governs and finds for Sanford.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYearMonth",
    "@value": "1854-05"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/dred-scott-v-sandford/timeline/8",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The U.S. Supreme Court hears the first oral argument; it orders re-argument, held in December 1856.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYearMonth",
    "@value": "1856-02"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/dred-scott-v-sandford/timeline/9",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "In his inaugural address, President James Buchanan says he will submit to the Court's coming decision.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1857-03-04"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/due-process/misconception",
   "@type": "uso:Misconception",
   "uso:fact": "The clauses protect against any government deprivation of life, liberty, or property, including civil and administrative actions such as cutting off government benefits (Goldberg v. Kelly, 1970; Mathews v. Eldridge, 1976), and courts weigh how much process each situation requires.",
   "uso:myth": "Due process is only about criminal trials.",
   "uso:why": "The most familiar procedural rights, such as indictment, jury trial and counsel, sit beside due process in the Fifth and Sixth Amendments, so people assume the clause belongs only to the courtroom."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/due-process/review/1",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why does the Constitution contain two Due Process Clauses?",
   "uso:answer": "The Fifth Amendment's clause was held in Barron v. Baltimore (1833) to bind only the federal government, so after the Civil War the Fourteenth Amendment (ratified 1868) added a parallel clause forbidding the states to deprive any person of life, liberty, or property without due process of law.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:due-process"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/due-process/review/2",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How does a court decide how much process is \"due\" when the government ends someone's benefits?",
   "uso:answer": "Under Mathews v. Eldridge (1976) it balances three factors, the private interest affected, the risk of an erroneous deprivation under the procedures used, and the government's interest, against a baseline of notice and an opportunity to be heard.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:due-process"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/due-process/review/3",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why is substantive due process more controversial than procedural due process or incorporation?",
   "uso:answer": "Critics argue the clause's text guarantees lawful procedure, not limits on what laws may say, and note the doctrine's roots in Dred Scott and Lochner; defenders answer that more than a century of precedent and the Ninth Amendment support protecting unenumerated rights, and that the real question is how judges should identify them.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:due-process"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/due-process/timeline/1",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Magna Carta promises that free men will be judged by their peers or by the law of the land.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1215"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/due-process/timeline/10",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Ratification of the Fourteenth Amendment extends a due process guarantee against the states.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1868-07-09"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/due-process/timeline/11",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Lochner v. New York strikes down a workplace regulation on \"freedom of contract\" grounds.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1905"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/due-process/timeline/12",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "West Coast Hotel Co. v. Parrish repudiates the Lochner approach to economic regulation.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1937"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/due-process/timeline/13",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Mullane v. Central Hanover Bank sets minimum requirements of notice, a hearing and an impartial tribunal.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1950"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/due-process/timeline/14",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Mathews v. Eldridge adopts a three-factor balancing test for the process due in civil proceedings.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1976"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/due-process/timeline/2",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "An English statute restating Magna Carta first uses the phrase \"due process of law.\"",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1354"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/due-process/timeline/3",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Sir Edward Coke's Second Institutes equates \"law of the land\" with \"due process of law,\" shaping the founders' understanding.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1641"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/due-process/timeline/4",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Maryland's and Virginia's new constitutions adopt Magna Carta's \"law of the land\" protections.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1776"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/due-process/timeline/5",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Congress proposes twelve amendments, including the article containing the Due Process Clause.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1789-09-25"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/due-process/timeline/6",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Ratification of the Bill of Rights adds the Fifth Amendment's Due Process Clause to the Constitution.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1791-12-15"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/due-process/timeline/7",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "New York becomes the first state to write \"due process of law\" into its own constitution.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1821"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/due-process/timeline/8",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Barron v. Baltimore holds that the Fifth Amendment and the rest of the Bill of Rights limit only the federal government.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1833"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/due-process/timeline/9",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "In Dred Scott the Court suggests that freeing an enslaved man would deprive his owner of property without due process.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1857"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/dust-bowl/misconception",
   "@type": "uso:Misconception",
   "uso:fact": "Dust Bowl refugees were only part of a migration of roughly half a million people from several Southern Plains states; many came from Arkansas, Missouri, eastern Oklahoma and East Texas, where tenant farmers were pushed off the land by falling prices, foreclosures and landlords using federal crop payments to mechanize and evict them.",
   "uso:myth": "The \"Okies\" who poured into California in the 1930s were mostly farm families blown out of the Dust Bowl.",
   "uso:why": "John Steinbeck's The Grapes of Wrath and Depression-era photographs fused the dust storms and the westward migration into one story, and Californians labelled all the newcomers \"Okies\"."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/dust-bowl/review/1",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why did drought in the 1930s produce such severe dust storms on the Southern Plains?",
   "uso:answer": "Between about 1909 and 1929 farmers had broken some 32 million acres of native sod, much of it for wheat; when drought killed the crops from 1931, bare plowed fields lost the grass roots that had held the soil, and high winds lifted it away.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:dust-bowl"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/dust-bowl/review/2",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How did the Dust Bowl change federal policy?",
   "uso:answer": "Dust clouds reaching Washington in 1934 and 1935 helped Hugh Hammond Bennett persuade Congress to pass the Soil Conservation Act of 1935, which created the permanent Soil Conservation Service; it promoted terracing, strip cropping and grass seeding and led to locally run soil conservation districts.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:dust-bowl"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/dust-bowl/review/3",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why do historians disagree about the causes of the Dust Bowl?",
   "uso:answer": "Donald Worster argued that a market-driven plow-up of marginal grassland made it a human-made ecological disaster, while later researchers using county data and climate models stress that dust storms are natural in long Plains droughts, that the heaviest wheat areas were not always the worst hit, and that ocean temperatures drove the drought.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:dust-bowl"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/dust-bowl/review/4",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Were most migrants who went to California from the Dust Bowl itself?",
   "uso:answer": "No. Of roughly half a million Southern Plains migrants, many were tenant farmers from Arkansas, Missouri, eastern Oklahoma and East Texas displaced by economic forces rather than drought.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:dust-bowl"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/dust-bowl/timeline/1",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Over the next two decades farmers break about 32 million acres of Great Plains sod, much of it for wheat.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1909"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/dust-bowl/timeline/10",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The first soil conservation district is organized, in North Carolina's Brown Creek watershed.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1937-08-04"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/dust-bowl/timeline/11",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "With near-normal rain returning from 1938, the drought ends.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1940"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/dust-bowl/timeline/2",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Drought ruins the Southern Plains wheat crop in the autumn, leaving fields bare.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1931"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/dust-bowl/timeline/3",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "By late January winds are lifting loose soil into dust storms.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYearMonth",
    "@value": "1932-01"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/dust-bowl/timeline/4",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Soil Erosion Service is set up in the Interior Department with Hugh Hammond Bennett as chief.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYearMonth",
    "@value": "1933-09"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/dust-bowl/timeline/5",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "A great dust storm carries Plains soil over Washington, D.C., and out over the Atlantic.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1934-05-11"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/dust-bowl/timeline/6",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "A second March dust cloud darkens Washington as Congress holds hearings on soil conservation.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1935-03-21"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/dust-bowl/timeline/7",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Black Sunday: a dust front sweeps from central Nebraska toward the Mexican border; a reporter's account names the Dust Bowl.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1935-04-14"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/dust-bowl/timeline/8",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Roosevelt signs the Soil Conservation Act, creating the permanent Soil Conservation Service in the Agriculture Department.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1935-04-27"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/dust-bowl/timeline/9",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "In a fireside chat Roosevelt reports on his tour of nine drought states and outlines work relief and land-use plans.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1936-09-06"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/e-pluribus-unum/misconception",
   "@type": "uso:Misconception",
   "uso:fact": "E pluribus unum has been the motto of the Great Seal since 1782 and is a required coin inscription, but no statute designated it the national motto; the 1956 act declaring \"In God we trust\" is the law that names one, and E pluribus unum stayed on the seal and coins.",
   "uso:myth": "E pluribus unum was the official national motto of the United States until 1956, when Congress replaced it with \"In God We Trust.\"",
   "uso:why": "The phrase appeared on the seal, coins and the dollar bill for so long that it was widely treated as the country's motto, and even an editor's note on the online edition of a 1923 scholarly study says it was replaced in 1956."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/e-pluribus-unum/review/1",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why does the Great Seal's motto describe \"many\" becoming \"one\", and what did the \"many\" originally mean?",
   "uso:answer": "In 1776 and 1782 the motto referred to the thirteen states joining in one union; Du Simitière's design ringed the shield with thirteen escutcheons for the states, and the official 1782 remarks say the motto alludes to the union of the states under Congress.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:e-pluribus-unum"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/e-pluribus-unum/review/2",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How did a motto from a London magazine end up on the seal, and why is it uncertain who chose it?",
   "uso:answer": "The phrase was printed on the title page of the widely read Gentleman's Magazine, which educated colonists knew. It first appears in the seal record in Du Simitière's August 1776 design for the Franklin-Adams-Jefferson committee, but no document says who suggested it, so the State Department credits Du Simitière, Deutsch argued for Franklin, and the Jefferson Papers editors leave it open.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:e-pluribus-unum"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/e-pluribus-unum/review/3",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why is it inaccurate to say \"In God We Trust\" replaced E pluribus unum as the national motto in 1956?",
   "uso:answer": "E pluribus unum had never been designated the national motto by law. The 1956 act was the first to declare a national motto, and E pluribus unum remained on the Great Seal and is still required by law on the reverse of every U.S. coin.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:e-pluribus-unum"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/e-pluribus-unum/timeline/1",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Pierre Motteux's London Gentleman's Journal carries the motto, the earliest printing Deutsch traced; the Gentleman's Magazine later puts it on its title page.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYearMonth",
    "@value": "1692-01"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/e-pluribus-unum/timeline/2",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Continental Congress appoints Benjamin Franklin, John Adams and Thomas Jefferson as a committee to design a seal.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1776-07-04"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/e-pluribus-unum/timeline/3",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The committee reports a design drawn up with artist Pierre Eugène Du Simitière bearing the motto E PLURIBUS UNUM; Congress tables it.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1776-08-20"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/e-pluribus-unum/timeline/4",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "A second committee, advised by Francis Hopkinson, adds the 13 stripes, 13 stars and olive branch but produces no accepted seal.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYearMonth",
    "@value": "1780-03"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/e-pluribus-unum/timeline/5",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Congress hands the work of all three committees to its secretary, Charles Thomson, who puts the motto on a scroll in the eagle's beak.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1782-06-13"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/e-pluribus-unum/timeline/6",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Confederation Congress adopts the Great Seal with the motto.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1782-06-20"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/e-pluribus-unum/timeline/7",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Franklin D. Roosevelt approves a one-dollar bill design showing both sides of the Great Seal, putting the motto on the back of the note.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1935"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/e-pluribus-unum/timeline/8",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "An act of Congress declares 'In God we trust' the national motto; E pluribus unum stays on the seal and coins.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1956-07-30"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/e-pluribus-unum/timeline/9",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Congress reenacts the national-motto statute without changing its wording.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "2002-11-13"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/electoral-college/misconception",
   "@type": "uso:Misconception",
   "uso:fact": "The phrase \"electoral college\" does not appear in the Constitution, and the electors never meet as one body; each state's electors meet in their own state in December, and Congress counts their certified votes in January.",
   "uso:myth": "The Constitution creates a body called the \"Electoral College\" whose members gather in one place to elect the president.",
   "uso:why": "The name suggests a single deliberative assembly, which hides how much of the process is run by the states and how little room individual electors now have."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/electoral-college/review/1",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why did the framers choose electors rather than election by Congress or by a national popular vote?",
   "uso:answer": "It was a compromise; selection by Congress risked making the president dependent on the legislature, while most delegates distrusted direct popular election, and Madison noted that a popular vote would disadvantage Southern states whose enslaved people could not vote.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:electoral-college"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/electoral-college/review/2",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How did the election of 1800 lead to the Twelfth Amendment?",
   "uso:answer": "Each elector cast two undifferentiated votes, so Jefferson and his running mate Burr tied at 73 and the House needed 36 ballots to choose; the amendment, ratified in 1804, gave electors separate votes for president and vice president.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:electoral-college"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/electoral-college/review/3",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How can a candidate win the national popular vote and still lose the presidency?",
   "uso:answer": "The president is chosen by state-allotted electors, and 48 states and the District of Columbia give all their electors to the statewide winner, so narrow wins in some states can outweigh large margins elsewhere, as in 1876, 1888, 2000 and 2016.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:electoral-college"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/electoral-college/review/4",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "What did the Supreme Court decide in Chiafalo v. Washington, and why?",
   "uso:answer": "It held unanimously in judgment in 2020 that states may enforce electors' pledges, reasoning from long practice that electors are not free agents but vote for the candidate their state's voters chose.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:electoral-college"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/electoral-college/timeline/1",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "At the Constitutional Convention, James Madison says direct popular election would leave the Southern states little influence because enslaved people could not vote, and leans toward electors.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1787-07-19"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/electoral-college/timeline/2",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Under the original two-vote rule, Federalist John Adams becomes president and his rival, Democratic-Republican Thomas Jefferson, vice president.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1796"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/electoral-college/timeline/3",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "After Jefferson and Aaron Burr tie at 73 electoral votes each, the House elects Jefferson on its 36th ballot.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1801-02-17"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/electoral-college/timeline/4",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "New Hampshire supplies the final ratification of the Twelfth Amendment, which gives electors separate votes for president and vice president.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYearMonth",
    "@value": "1804-06"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/electoral-college/timeline/5",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "With no electoral majority, the House chooses John Quincy Adams over Andrew Jackson, who had led in electoral and popular votes.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1825-02-09"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/electoral-college/timeline/6",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "By this year almost every state has its voters, rather than its legislature, choose presidential electors.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1832"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/electoral-college/timeline/7",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "After Richard M. Johnson falls one vote short of an electoral majority for vice president, the Senate elects him 33 to 16, the only time it has chosen a vice president.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1836"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/electoral-college/timeline/8",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "In Chiafalo v. Washington, the Supreme Court rules that states may enforce electors' pledges to vote for their party's nominee.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "2020-07-06"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/equal-protection/misconception",
   "@type": "uso:Misconception",
   "uso:fact": "Governments classify people constantly, by age, income, or occupation, and most such lines stand if they are \"rationally related to a legitimate state interest\"; only suspect classifications such as race, or laws burdening fundamental rights, face the demanding test of strict scrutiny.",
   "uso:myth": "The Equal Protection Clause forbids government from ever treating people differently.",
   "uso:why": "Equal protection requires equal treatment of those who are \"similarly situated,\" not identical treatment of everyone, so the real question a court asks is which differences a law may lawfully draw on."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/equal-protection/review/1",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why did the framers of the Fourteenth Amendment write an equal protection guarantee into the Constitution rather than rely on ordinary legislation?",
   "uso:answer": "They wanted to give civil rights measures such as the Civil Rights Act of 1866 a constitutional footing that a later simple majority in Congress could not repeal, so that the equal legal standing of formerly enslaved people could not be undone by shifting politics.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:equal-protection"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/equal-protection/review/2",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How can two laws that both classify people be judged so differently under the Equal Protection Clause?",
   "uso:answer": "Courts apply different levels of review depending on the classification. Most laws need only a rational relationship to a legitimate interest, but a law that sorts people by race (a suspect classification) or burdens a fundamental right must survive strict scrutiny, meaning it must be narrowly tailored to a compelling government interest.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:equal-protection"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/equal-protection/timeline/1",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The House approves the proposed Fourteenth Amendment, 120-32, carrying the equal protection language.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1866-06-13"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/equal-protection/timeline/2",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Ratification of the Fourteenth Amendment writes the Equal Protection Clause into the Constitution.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1868-07-09"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/equal-protection/timeline/3",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Slaughter-House Cases recognize formerly enslaved African Americans as the clause's primary intended beneficiaries while noting its words reach everyone.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1873"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/equal-protection/timeline/4",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Yick Wo v. Hopkins applies equal protection beyond race to discriminatory enforcement of an ordinance.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1886"
   }
  },
  {
