  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/great-railroad-strike-of-1877/misconception",
   "@type": "uso:Misconception",
   "uso:fact": "The walkout began on the Baltimore and Ohio Railroad and spread largely on its own along the rail lines; almost none of the strikers belonged to a union and no leaders or organization controlled the strike as a whole, although local groups such as a railroad firemen's organization in West Virginia and socialists in Chicago and St. Louis played real roles in particular places.",
   "uso:myth": "The 1877 strike was a planned national uprising, organized and directed by labor unions or radical leaders.",
   "uso:why": "The strike's sudden national reach and the visible part socialists played in a few cities made it look coordinated, and treating it as a conspiracy obscures the wage cuts and hard times that drove ordinary workers and their neighbours into the streets."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/great-railroad-strike-of-1877/review/1",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why did a wage cut on one railroad turn into a crisis that stretched from Baltimore to St. Louis?",
   "uso:answer": "Railroad workers had already suffered years of depression and repeated wage cuts since 1873, so when the Baltimore and Ohio announced another ten percent cut, the walkout at Martinsburg spread along the connected trunk lines, drew in unemployed people and workers in other trades, and local police and militias could not or would not stop it.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:great-railroad-strike-of-1877"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/great-railroad-strike-of-1877/review/2",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How did the federal government become involved, and what limits did President Hayes set on that role?",
   "uso:answer": "Governors who said they could not keep order formally asked for federal help, and Hayes issued proclamations and sent Army troops; he refused the Pennsylvania Railroad's request to act directly for the companies and did not use soldiers to run trains, describing his aim as restoring order, though the deployment set a precedent for later presidents.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:great-railroad-strike-of-1877"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/great-railroad-strike-of-1877/review/3",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "What lessons did workers and employers each draw from the strike?",
   "uso:answer": "Many workers concluded they needed lasting, organized unions, while businesses concluded they needed more political influence and government help, including better-prepared state forces to deal with future labor unrest.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:great-railroad-strike-of-1877"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/great-railroad-strike-of-1877/timeline/1",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The railroads' financial bubble bursts, beginning years of depression, layoffs and repeated wage cuts.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1873"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/great-railroad-strike-of-1877/timeline/2",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Railroad workers at Martinsburg, West Virginia, walk off the job over a ten percent wage cut on the Baltimore and Ohio Railroad and stop trains.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1877-07-16"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/great-railroad-strike-of-1877/timeline/3",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Governor Henry M. Mathews asks for federal help, and President Rutherford B. Hayes proclaims that those obstructing the railroad must disperse by noon the next day.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1877-07-18"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/great-railroad-strike-of-1877/timeline/4",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Federal troops arrive at Martinsburg and end the violence there.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1877-07-19"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/great-railroad-strike-of-1877/timeline/5",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Hayes issues a similar proclamation for Maryland; in Pittsburgh, militia from Philadelphia fire into a crowd near the 28th Street crossing, killing about twenty people.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1877-07-21"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/great-railroad-strike-of-1877/timeline/6",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Fires in Pittsburgh destroy the Pennsylvania Railroad's Union Depot, more than thirty buildings and thousands of locomotives and cars.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1877-07-22"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/great-railroad-strike-of-1877/timeline/7",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Unrest in Chicago peaks over five days of walkouts, sympathy strikes and clashes with police, militia and U.S. Army troops.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1877-07-24"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/great-railroad-strike-of-1877/timeline/8",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "By the end of July the strikes have largely been put down along the trunk lines.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1877-07-31"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/great-railroad-strike-of-1877/timeline/9",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "By early 1880 the railroads have restored the wages they had cut.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1880"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/great-seal-of-the-united-states/misconception",
   "@type": "uso:Misconception",
   "uso:fact": "The Eye of Providence came from artist Pierre Eugène Du Simitière's 1776 design for the first seal committee, the pyramid from William Barton's 1782 reverse, and Charles Thomson finished both; Franklin is the only known Freemason involved at any stage, and the eye-over-pyramid has never been adopted as a symbol by any grand lodge.",
   "uso:myth": "The pyramid and all-seeing eye on the back of the dollar bill are a Masonic emblem that Freemason founders put on the Great Seal.",
   "uso:why": "Some Masonic lodges have used an eye as a design element, and the reverse, never cut as a die, became familiar through the 1935 one-dollar bill rather than through Thomson's 1782 explanation of its religious and patriotic meaning."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/great-seal-of-the-united-states/review/1",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why did it take six years and three committees to produce the Great Seal?",
   "uso:answer": "The 1776 committee's elaborate design, with emblems of European nations and a Red Sea scene, was tabled; a 1780 committee's report was recommitted; only in 1782 did Congress give the material of all three committees to Charles Thomson, who combined elements such as the 1776 eye and motto and Barton's eagle and pyramid into a design Congress accepted within a week.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:great-seal-of-the-united-states"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/great-seal-of-the-united-states/review/2",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How does the front of the seal express the powers Congress held under the Articles of Confederation?",
   "uso:answer": "The eagle holds an olive branch and thirteen arrows, standing for the powers of peace and war, which under the Confederation belonged to Congress alone; the shield's thirteen stripes for the states are joined under a blue chief representing Congress.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:great-seal-of-the-united-states"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/great-seal-of-the-united-states/review/3",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why does the reverse of the seal appear on the dollar bill but never on an official document?",
   "uso:answer": "No die for the reverse has ever been cut, so documents are sealed only with the obverse; the reverse became widely seen after Roosevelt approved a 1935 dollar-bill design that printed both sides.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:great-seal-of-the-united-states"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/great-seal-of-the-united-states/review/4",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "What evidence contradicts the claim that the eye and pyramid are a Masonic emblem?",
   "uso:answer": "The design record traces the eye to the non-Mason artist Du Simitière in 1776 and the pyramid to Barton in 1782, with Thomson finishing the reverse; Franklin was the only known Mason involved, and no grand lodge has adopted the eye over a pyramid as a symbol.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:great-seal-of-the-united-states"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/great-seal-of-the-united-states/timeline/1",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Congress names Franklin, Adams and Jefferson a committee to devise a seal for the new nation.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1776-07-04"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/great-seal-of-the-united-states/timeline/10",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "A Tiffany & Co. die corrects the errors of the 1841 and 1877 dies.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1885"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/great-seal-of-the-united-states/timeline/11",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Franklin D. Roosevelt approves a one-dollar bill showing both sides of the seal.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1935"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/great-seal-of-the-united-states/timeline/12",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Bureau of Engraving and Printing makes the master die from which current dies are produced.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1986"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/great-seal-of-the-united-states/timeline/2",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The first committee's report, with the Eye of Providence and the motto E pluribus unum, is read and tabled.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1776-08-20"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/great-seal-of-the-united-states/timeline/3",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "A second committee, advised by Francis Hopkinson, takes up the design; its report is recommitted in May.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1780-03-25"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/great-seal-of-the-united-states/timeline/4",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "A third committee turns to Philadelphia lawyer William Barton, who drafts an eagle and a thirteen-step pyramid.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYearMonth",
    "@value": "1782-05"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/great-seal-of-the-united-states/timeline/5",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Congress hands all three committees' material to its secretary, Charles Thomson.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1782-06-13"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/great-seal-of-the-united-states/timeline/6",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Congress adopts Thomson's design and his written explanation of its symbols.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1782-06-20"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/great-seal-of-the-united-states/timeline/7",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The first brass die is used on a document empowering George Washington to negotiate over prisoners of war.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1782-09-16"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/great-seal-of-the-united-states/timeline/8",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "An act of Congress renames the Department of Foreign Affairs the Department of State and makes the Secretary of State keeper of the seal.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1789-09-15"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/great-seal-of-the-united-states/timeline/9",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "A new steel die with only six arrows and five-pointed stars departs from the law and is later called the illegal seal.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1841"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/harriet-tubman/misconception",
   "@type": "uso:Misconception",
   "uso:fact": "Records and her own accounts show about 13 trips, all to Maryland's Eastern Shore, freeing about 70 people, while she gave directions to about 70 more who escaped on their own. The only documented reward was $100 each for her and two brothers in 1849, offered by slaveholders who did not know she was Tubman.",
   "uso:myth": "Tubman made 19 trips into the South and rescued more than 300 people, with a $40,000 bounty on her head.",
   "uso:why": "The larger numbers come from Sarah Bradford's 1868–69 biography and an 1867 letter supporting her pension claim; they stuck because they suited a legend, but they obscure how carefully she chose people she knew and trusted."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/harriet-tubman/review/1",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why did Tubman return only to Maryland's Eastern Shore rather than travel all over the South?",
   "uso:answer": "Her missions were to bring away family members and friends she knew and trusted; she knew that landscape from her years of labor in its marshes and woods, and unfamiliar places were far more dangerous.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:harriet-tubman"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/harriet-tubman/review/2",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How did the Fugitive Slave Act of 1850 change where Tubman took the people she rescued?",
   "uso:answer": "The 1850 law put any freedom seeker in the United States, even in the North, at risk of recapture, so she guided people beyond Philadelphia to St. Catharines, Ontario, in Canada.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:harriet-tubman"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/harriet-tubman/review/3",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why did Tubman have to petition Congress for a pension, and what did she get?",
   "uso:answer": "She had served the Union as a nurse, cook, scout and spy without formal enlistment or regular pay, and at first received only an $8-a-month widow's pension as Nelson Davis's widow; in 1899 Congress raised it to $20 a month for her service as a nurse.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:harriet-tubman"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/harriet-tubman/timeline/1",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Born Araminta \"Minty\" Ross, probably in February or early March, on Anthony Thompson's plantation at Peter's Neck, Dorchester County, Maryland.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1822"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/harriet-tubman/timeline/10",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Arrives at Port Royal, South Carolina, to serve the Union Army as a scout, spy, cook and nurse.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1862"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/harriet-tubman/timeline/11",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Helps lead the Combahee River raid with Colonel James Montgomery, freeing more than 700 enslaved people.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1863-06-02"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/harriet-tubman/timeline/12",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Sarah Bradford publishes the first biography of Tubman; Tubman marries Civil War veteran Nelson Davis in Auburn.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1869"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/harriet-tubman/timeline/13",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Congress passes H.R. 4982, raising her pension to $20 a month in recognition of her service as an army nurse.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1899-01-19"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/harriet-tubman/timeline/14",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The AME Zion Church opens the Harriet Tubman Home for aged and poor African Americans on land she had transferred to it in 1903.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1908"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/harriet-tubman/timeline/15",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Dies of pneumonia in Auburn and is buried at Fort Hill Cemetery.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1913-03-10"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/harriet-tubman/timeline/2",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Probably marries John Tubman, a free Black man, and takes the name Harriet.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1844"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/harriet-tubman/timeline/3",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "After her enslaver Edward Brodess dies in March, she fears being sold, escapes alone in the late fall and reaches Philadelphia.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1849"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/harriet-tubman/timeline/4",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Congress passes the Fugitive Slave Act; Tubman makes her first rescue, of her niece Kessiah and Kessiah's two children.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1850"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/harriet-tubman/timeline/5",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Rescues her three remaining brothers from Dorchester County.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1854-12-25"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/harriet-tubman/timeline/6",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Brings her aging parents north after learning her father faces arrest.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1857"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/harriet-tubman/timeline/7",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Meets John Brown at St. Catharines, Ontario, and recruits support for his plans, though she does not join the 1859 Harpers Ferry raid.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1858"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/harriet-tubman/timeline/8",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Buys a house and seven acres near Auburn, New York, from Senator William H. Seward.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYearMonth",
    "@value": "1859-05"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/harriet-tubman/timeline/9",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Last rescue mission to Maryland brings away seven people.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYearMonth",
    "@value": "1860-12"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/haudenosaunee/misconception",
   "@type": "uso:Misconception",
   "uso:fact": "Some framers knew and admired the Six Nations' union, and Congress formally acknowledged the confederacy's influence in 1988, but historians disagree about how much it shaped the Constitution; the two systems also differ sharply, since Haudenosaunee clan mothers select chiefs and the Grand Council decides by consensus rather than by elections and majority votes.",
   "uso:myth": "The framers copied the United States Constitution from the Haudenosaunee Great Law of Peace.",
   "uso:why": "The story is a corrective to histories that left Native nations out of the founding, and real documents such as Franklin's 1751 letter seem to confirm it, so an argument about influence is easily remembered as a claim of direct copying."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/haudenosaunee/review/1",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How does the Haudenosaunee Grand Council reach a decision?",
   "uso:answer": "The 50 chiefs sit on three benches — the Mohawk and Seneca as Older Brothers, the Oneida and Cayuga as Younger Brothers, and the Onondaga as Firekeepers — and each bench must agree before passing a matter on; a decision is made only when all three agree, which the Onondaga Nation calls reaching \"One Mind.\"",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:haudenosaunee"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/haudenosaunee/review/2",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why did the American Revolution devastate the Haudenosaunee even though one of its nations fought on the winning side?",
   "uso:answer": "The confederacy split, with the Oneida and Tuscarora supporting the Americans and most of the other nations joining Britain; American forces burned many Seneca, Onondaga and Cayuga towns in 1779, an Oneida village was destroyed in 1780, and after the war New York's land purchases shrank even the Oneida homeland despite the 1784 Fort Stanwix guarantee.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:haudenosaunee"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/haudenosaunee/review/3",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How did the Kinzua Dam conflict with the Treaty of Canandaigua?",
   "uso:answer": "In 1794 the United States promised never to claim or disturb the Seneca lands it recognized, yet the federal government took Allegany Reservation land by eminent domain for the dam, flooding about 10,000 acres and moving about 700 people, after courts declined to stop it.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:haudenosaunee"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/haudenosaunee/review/4",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why do historians disagree about whether the Haudenosaunee influenced the Constitution?",
   "uso:answer": "Scholars such as Donald Grinde and Bruce Johansen point to Canasatego's 1744 call for colonial union and Franklin's 1751 letter as evidence of influence, while Elisabeth Tooker argued the evidence is thin, that European theory supplied the framers' models, and that the League's matrilineal, consensus-based structure differs greatly from the federal system.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:haudenosaunee"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/haudenosaunee/timeline/1",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Tuscarora, who had moved north from North Carolina and Virginia, join the confederacy, which becomes the Six Nations.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1722"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/haudenosaunee/timeline/10",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Congress agrees to H. Con. Res. 331, acknowledging the contributions of the Iroquois Confederacy and other Indian nations to the formation of the United States.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1988-10-21"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/haudenosaunee/timeline/11",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The wampum belts taken in 1890 are returned to the Onondaga Nation.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1989"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/haudenosaunee/timeline/2",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Benjamin Franklin, urging a union of the British colonies, points to the Six Nations' union as proof that one is possible.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1751-03-20"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/haudenosaunee/timeline/3",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Boundary Line Treaty at Fort Stanwix pushes the colonial boundary deep into Oneida land.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1768"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/haudenosaunee/timeline/4",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Oneida warriors fight beside New York militia at Oriskany while most of the Six Nations side with Britain, splitting the confederacy.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1777-08-06"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/haudenosaunee/timeline/5",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Clinton-Sullivan expedition burns many Seneca, Onondaga and Cayuga towns.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1779"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/haudenosaunee/timeline/6",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Treaty of Fort Stanwix affirms that the Haudenosaunee and the United States are separate nations and sets boundaries to stop settlement.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1784"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/haudenosaunee/timeline/7",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Treaty of Canandaigua establishes peace and friendship and recognizes Seneca, Oneida, Onondaga and Cayuga lands.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1794-11-11"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/haudenosaunee/timeline/8",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "New York State takes the Onondaga Nation's wampum belts.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1890"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/haudenosaunee/timeline/9",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Kinzua Dam on the Allegheny River is completed, flooding about 10,000 acres of the Seneca Allegany Reservation and forcing about 700 people to move.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1965"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/hawaii/misconception",
   "@type": "uso:Misconception",
   "uso:fact": "More than 21,000 Native Hawaiians—over half of those counted in 1897—signed petitions against annexation, and the 1897 treaty died in the Senate with only 46 supporters; annexation came instead by a joint resolution that needed only simple majorities, signed July 7, 1898.",
   "uso:myth": "Native Hawaiians accepted annexation, and Hawaiʻi joined the United States through a ratified treaty.",
   "uso:why": "The petitions were long absent from Hawaiian history books and few people knew they existed until a scholar found them in the National Archives, while the 1959 statehood referendum is easily read back onto 1898."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/hawaii/review/1",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why was Hawaiʻi annexed by a joint resolution of Congress rather than by treaty?",
   "uso:answer": "A treaty needed a two-thirds Senate vote, and after more than 21,000 Native Hawaiians petitioned against annexation, only 46 senators would support the 1897 treaty. When the Spanish–American War revived interest in the islands, supporters used a joint resolution, which needed only simple majorities, and McKinley signed it on July 7, 1898.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:hawaii"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/hawaii/review/2",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How did the sugar plantation economy change who lived in Hawaiʻi?",
   "uso:answer": "Planters recruited contract laborers from Japan, China, the Philippines, Portugal and elsewhere, while disease after Western contact sharply reduced the Native Hawaiian population. Native Hawaiians fell from 97 percent of the population in 1853 to 16 percent in 1923, when people of Japanese ancestry were the largest group.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:hawaii"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/hawaii/review/3",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why did statehood take until 1959, and how was it tied to Alaska?",
   "uso:answer": "Territorial requests for statehood began in 1903 and a 1937 congressional committee found Hawaiʻi qualified, but plantation interests, the Pearl Harbor attack and Senate inaction delayed it. Congress paired Hawaiʻi with Alaska; once Alaska was admitted in early 1959, Hawaiʻi's bill passed in March, voters accepted it in June, and it became the fiftieth state on August 21, 1959.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:hawaii"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/hawaii/review/4",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "What does the 1993 Apology Resolution do, and what does it not do?",
   "uso:answer": "It acknowledges that U.S. agents and citizens took part in the 1893 overthrow and apologizes for depriving Native Hawaiians of self-determination, but it states that it does not settle any claims against the United States.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:hawaii"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/hawaii/timeline/1",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "By about 1000–1200 CE, according to recent carbon dating, Polynesian voyagers from the Marquesas have settled the islands; later voyagers come from the Society Islands.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1000"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/hawaii/timeline/10",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Members of Hui Aloha ʻĀina sign petitions against annexation; the drive gathers 21,269 Native Hawaiian signatures.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1897-09-11"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/hawaii/timeline/11",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "President McKinley signs the Newlands Resolution annexing Hawaiʻi by joint resolution, after an annexation treaty failed in the Senate.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1898-07-07"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/hawaii/timeline/12",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The United States formally annexes Hawaiʻi.",
   "uso:date": {
