  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/japanese-american-incarceration/misconception",
   "@type": "uso:Misconception",
   "uso:fact": "No Japanese American was convicted of serious espionage or sabotage during the war; the rumours cited by the Army were unverified, and Congress later found the policy was driven by racial prejudice, war hysteria and a failure of political leadership.",
   "uso:myth": "Japanese Americans were removed and imprisoned because they were caught spying or committing sabotage, so the camps were a military necessity.",
   "uso:why": "The government justified the removal as military necessity at the time, the Supreme Court accepted that claim in 1944, and the word \"evacuation\" made forced removal sound like protection."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/japanese-american-incarceration/review/1",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why do the National Park Service and many historians prefer \"incarceration\" and \"forced removal\" to \"internment\" and \"evacuation\"?",
   "uso:answer": "Internment is a law-of-war term for detaining enemy nationals, which the Justice Department and Army did separately to non-citizens; most people in the ten WRA camps were U.S. citizens held without charges, and \"evacuation\" wrongly suggests being moved out of danger.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:japanese-american-incarceration"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/japanese-american-incarceration/review/2",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How did the government justify the removal, and what does the evidence show?",
   "uso:answer": "General DeWitt and President Roosevelt claimed military necessity, citing reports of signals and sabotage; those reports were unverified, the FBI's director mocked the Army's intelligence as hysterical, and no Japanese American was convicted of serious espionage or sabotage.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:japanese-american-incarceration"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/japanese-american-incarceration/review/3",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How did the Supreme Court's two December 1944 decisions differ?",
   "uso:answer": "In Korematsu it upheld the exclusion order 6–3 as a military necessity, while in Ex parte Endo it ruled that the government could not keep detaining citizens it conceded were loyal; the exclusion orders were lifted effective January 2, 1945.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:japanese-american-incarceration"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/japanese-american-incarceration/review/4",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "What did the redress movement achieve, and how?",
   "uso:answer": "Japanese American organizations pressed Congress, which created a commission in 1980; its findings and recommendations led to the Civil Liberties Act of 1988, which apologized, paid $20,000 to each surviving incarcerated person (82,219 people in all) and funded public education.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:japanese-american-incarceration"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/japanese-american-incarceration/timeline/1",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Japan attacks Pearl Harbor; a presidential proclamation makes Japanese nationals in the U.S. liable to be detained as enemy aliens.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1941-12-07"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/japanese-american-incarceration/timeline/10",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The West Coast exclusion orders are lifted.",
