  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/mcculloch-v-maryland/misconception",
   "@type": "uso:Misconception",
   "uso:fact": "Marshall's opinion restated that the national government is one of enumerated powers; it allowed only means clearly suited to a legitimate end within the Constitution's scope, and it said the Court would have to declare void a law passed under the pretext of executing a power for objects not entrusted to the government.",
   "uso:myth": "McCulloch v. Maryland gave Congress unlimited power to pass any law it considers useful.",
   "uso:why": "Reading the case as a blank check obscures the real argument it started, which is about how far implied means can reach, not whether limits exist at all."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/mcculloch-v-maryland/review/1",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why did Marshall reject Maryland's argument that \"necessary\" in the Necessary and Proper Clause meant \"absolutely necessary\"?",
   "uso:answer": "He said the word admits of degrees and is commonly used to mean convenient, useful or conducive to an end, and he pointed out that Article I, Section 10 uses the phrase \"absolutely necessary,\" which showed the framers knew how to demand strict necessity when they wanted it; a narrow reading would also leave the government unable to carry out its purposes.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:mcculloch-v-maryland"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/mcculloch-v-maryland/review/2",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How did the idea that \"the power to tax involves the power to destroy\" lead the Court to strike down Maryland's tax?",
   "uso:answer": "Because the national government derives its authority from the people of the whole Union and is supreme within its sphere, a single state cannot use its taxing power to burden or control the means the national government uses; allowing such a tax would let a state defeat what Congress had created.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:mcculloch-v-maryland"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/mcculloch-v-maryland/review/3",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How did Andrew Jackson's 1832 bank veto challenge the authority of the McCulloch decision?",
   "uso:answer": "Jackson argued that each officer swears to support the Constitution as he understands it, that the Court's opinion must not control Congress or the President, and that whether the bank's particular features were necessary and proper was for the political branches to judge, so he could veto the recharter even though the Court had upheld the bank.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:mcculloch-v-maryland"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/mcculloch-v-maryland/review/4",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "What did James Madison object to in the decision, even though he had signed the Second Bank's charter in 1816?",
   "uso:answer": "He objected to the Court's broad rule of construction, warning that letting Congress treat almost anything as a means to an enumerated end could erase the limits set by enumerating its powers, and that few ratifiers would have accepted so pliant a rule.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:mcculloch-v-maryland"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/mcculloch-v-maryland/timeline/1",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "President James Madison signs the act chartering the Second Bank of the United States for twenty years.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYearMonth",
    "@value": "1816-04"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/mcculloch-v-maryland/timeline/10",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Second Bank's federal charter expires without renewal.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1836"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/mcculloch-v-maryland/timeline/2",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Second Bank opens in Philadelphia; it later sets up branches, including one in Baltimore.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYearMonth",
    "@value": "1817-01"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/mcculloch-v-maryland/timeline/3",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Maryland's legislature passes a stamp tax on banks operating in the state without a state charter, effective May 1, 1818.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1818-02-11"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/mcculloch-v-maryland/timeline/4",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Maryland tax takes effect; James W. McCulloch, cashier of the Baltimore branch, later issues notes without the required stamps.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1818-05-01"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/mcculloch-v-maryland/timeline/5",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Supreme Court unanimously rules for McCulloch, upholding the bank and striking down Maryland's tax.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1819-03-06"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/mcculloch-v-maryland/timeline/6",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Virginia judge Spencer Roane begins his four 'Hampden' essays attacking the decision in the Richmond Enquirer.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1819-06-11"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/mcculloch-v-maryland/timeline/7",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "James Madison writes to Roane objecting to the breadth of the Court's rule of construction.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1819-09-02"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/mcculloch-v-maryland/timeline/8",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "In Osborn v. Bank of the United States the Court holds that Congress may give a national bank the powers essential to its operation.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1824"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/mcculloch-v-maryland/timeline/9",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "President Andrew Jackson vetoes a bill to recharter the bank, arguing the Court's view does not bind Congress or the President.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1832-07-10"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/mexican-american-war/misconception",
   "@type": "uso:Misconception",
   "uso:fact": "Lincoln voted for a House amendment declaring the war unnecessarily and unconstitutionally begun, but he wrote in February 1848 that he had always intended to vote supplies for the army.",
   "uso:myth": "Abraham Lincoln opposed the Mexican War so strongly that he refused to vote money and supplies for the soldiers fighting it.",
   "uso:why": "Merging his attack on Polk's justification with a refusal to support the troops erases a distinction Lincoln drew deliberately in explaining his votes."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/mexican-american-war/review/1",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why did the land between the Nueces River and the Rio Grande matter so much in starting the war?",
   "uso:answer": "The United States, inheriting Texas's claim, said the border was the Rio Grande, while Mexico and many Americans held that the strip had never been part of Texas. When Polk sent Taylor's army into it and Mexican troops attacked a U.S. patrol there, Polk could tell Congress that American blood had been shed on American soil, while Mexico saw an invasion.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:mexican-american-war"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/mexican-american-war/review/2",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How did victory over Mexico make the conflict over slavery worse rather than settling it?",
   "uso:answer": "The huge Mexican Cession reopened the question of whether slavery would spread into new territories. The Wilmot Proviso to ban slavery there passed the House but failed in the Senate, deepening sectional division that the Compromise of 1850 only temporarily eased and that fed into the Civil War.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:mexican-american-war"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/mexican-american-war/review/3",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "What did Lincoln's 'Spot' Resolutions challenge, and did he refuse to support the army?",
   "uso:answer": "They demanded that Polk identify the exact spot where American blood was first shed and show it was U.S. soil; the House never acted on them. Lincoln also voted that the war had been unconstitutionally begun, but he said he always intended to vote supplies for the troops.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:mexican-american-war"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/mexican-american-war/review/4",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How, according to historian Brian DeLay, did Native raids shape the U.S. invasion of northern Mexico?",
   "uso:answer": "Some fifteen years of raiding and counter-raiding across ten northern Mexican states had killed thousands and depopulated the countryside, strengthening U.S. arguments for taking the territory and leaving northern Mexicans too divided and exhausted to resist the American invasion effectively.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:mexican-american-war"
   }
  },
  {
