  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/nato/misconception",
   "@type": "uso:Misconception",
   "uso:fact": "Article 5 treats an attack on one as an attack on all, but each member takes \"such action as it deems necessary,\" which may or may not include armed force; U.S. negotiators insisted on that wording because the Constitution gives Congress the power to declare war.",
   "uso:myth": "Article 5 automatically obliges every NATO member to go to war when an ally is attacked.",
   "uso:why": "The slogan \"an attack on one is an attack on all\" is remembered without the clause that leaves each government to decide its own response."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/nato/review/1",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why did the United States refuse to write an automatic military commitment into the North Atlantic Treaty?",
   "uso:answer": "European negotiators wanted a guarantee of automatic U.S. intervention, but under the Constitution only Congress can declare war, so Article 5 lets each member take the action it deems necessary, including but not limited to armed force.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:nato"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/nato/review/2",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How did the Vandenberg Resolution prepare the way for NATO?",
   "uso:answer": "Adopted by the Senate in June 1948, it endorsed U.S. association with regional collective-defense arrangements under the UN Charter and through constitutional processes, giving the administration bipartisan Senate backing to negotiate the treaty.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:nato"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/nato/review/3",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why was joining NATO a break with earlier American foreign policy?",
   "uso:answer": "It was the first peacetime military alliance the United States entered outside the Western Hemisphere, ending a long tradition of avoiding alliances with European powers.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:nato"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/nato/review/4",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "What did the only invocation of Article 5 lead to?",
   "uso:answer": "After the September 11, 2001, attacks the allies invoked Article 5 on September 12; NATO then agreed eight support measures and sent AWACS radar planes to patrol U.S. skies in Operation Eagle Assist, its first Article 5 operation.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:nato"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/nato/timeline/1",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Britain, France, Belgium, the Netherlands and Luxembourg sign the Brussels Treaty, a five-nation collective defense pact.",
   "uso:date": {
