   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/presidency-of-the-united-states/timeline/6",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Twentieth Amendment is ratified, moving the end of presidential terms to noon on January 20.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1933-01-23"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/presidency-of-the-united-states/timeline/7",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Brownlow Committee reports that the president needs more staff help.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1937"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/presidency-of-the-united-states/timeline/8",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Franklin D. Roosevelt creates the Executive Office of the President.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1939"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/presidency-of-the-united-states/timeline/9",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Twenty-second Amendment is ratified, limiting presidents to two elected terms.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1951-02-27"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/reconstruction-era/misconception",
   "@type": "uso:Misconception",
   "uso:fact": "This \"tragic era\" story, spread by William Dunning's Columbia school, Claude Bowers's The Tragic Era and films, has been rejected by historians; Southern corruption was no worse than national scandals, Black men held a small share of offices, and the Reconstruction governments built the South's first public school systems before being overthrown by violence.",
   "uso:myth": "Reconstruction failed because vindictive Radicals handed the South to corrupt carpetbaggers and to freedmen unfit to govern, until white \"redeemers\" restored honest rule.",
   "uso:why": "The legend served as intellectual justification for segregation and disfranchisement for much of the twentieth century, so correcting it changes how Americans understand Jim Crow and the civil rights movement."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/reconstruction-era/review/1",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why did Congress replace Andrew Johnson's plan with Congressional (Radical) Reconstruction in 1867?",
   "uso:answer": "Under Johnson's lenient plan, Southern states passed Black Codes that restricted freedpeople's movement and labor, and Johnson vetoed civil-rights measures, so Republican majorities overrode him, passed the Fourteenth Amendment and the Reconstruction Acts, and required new state constitutions with Black male suffrage before readmission.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:reconstruction-era"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/reconstruction-era/review/2",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How did violence and economic depression help end Reconstruction?",
   "uso:answer": "The Klan and other paramilitaries terrorized Black voters and Republicans, as at Colfax in 1873, while the Panic of 1873 turned Northern attention to the economy and helped Democrats win the House in 1874; federal will to enforce Black rights faded, and the Compromise of 1877 withdrew the last troops.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:reconstruction-era"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/reconstruction-era/review/3",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why do historians call Reconstruction a \"splendid failure\" rather than simply a failure?",
   "uso:answer": "It failed to redistribute land or protect Black voting, and planters regained power, yet it wrote birthright citizenship, equal protection and Black male suffrage into the Constitution, and those amendments later became the legal basis of the civil rights movement.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:reconstruction-era"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/reconstruction-era/timeline/1",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "In the fall, President Abraham Lincoln offers amnesty and lets loyal Southerners form new state governments once 10 percent of voters swear an oath of loyalty.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1863"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/reconstruction-era/timeline/10",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Ku Klux Klan Act lets federal courts try crimes against freedpeople and allows suspension of habeas corpus.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1871"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/reconstruction-era/timeline/11",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "White paramilitaries kill as many as 150 Black men at Colfax, Louisiana; in September the Panic of 1873 opens a long depression.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1873"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/reconstruction-era/timeline/12",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Congress passes a Civil Rights Act requiring equal treatment in public places and on juries.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1875"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/reconstruction-era/timeline/13",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "An 8–7 electoral commission vote and the Compromise of 1877 seat Rutherford B. Hayes; federal troops are withdrawn from the South in April.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1877"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/reconstruction-era/timeline/2",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Congress passes the Thirteenth Amendment abolishing slavery; it is ratified by the end of the year.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1865-01-31"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/reconstruction-era/timeline/3",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Lincoln is shot and dies the next morning; Andrew Johnson becomes president and begins a lenient Presidential Reconstruction.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1865-04-14"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/reconstruction-era/timeline/4",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Congress passes the Civil Rights Act of 1866 over Johnson's veto; the Ku Klux Klan is organized in Pulaski, Tennessee; deadly riots against Black residents erupt in Memphis and New Orleans.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1866"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/reconstruction-era/timeline/5",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Congress passes the Fourteenth Amendment, guaranteeing birthright citizenship, due process and equal protection.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1866-06-13"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/reconstruction-era/timeline/6",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The first Reconstruction Act becomes law over Johnson's veto, placing the former Confederate states except Tennessee under five military districts.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1867-03-02"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/reconstruction-era/timeline/7",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Arkansas becomes the first state readmitted to congressional representation under the Reconstruction Acts.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1868-06-22"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/reconstruction-era/timeline/8",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Fourteenth Amendment is certified as ratified by 28 of 37 states.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1868-07-28"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/reconstruction-era/timeline/9",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "A Baltimore parade celebrates the Fifteenth Amendment, which bars racial discrimination in voting; the same year Congress passes the first Enforcement Act against intimidation at the polls.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1870-05-19"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/reynolds-v-sims/misconception",
   "@type": "uso:Misconception",
   "uso:fact": "The phrase entered the Supreme Court's case law a year earlier, in Gray v. Sanders (1963), which struck down Georgia's county-unit system for statewide elections; Reynolds applied that equality standard to the seats of both houses of state legislatures.",
   "uso:myth": "Reynolds v. Sims coined the phrase \"one person, one vote.\"",
   "uso:why": "Because Reynolds reshaped every state legislature, it became the case most associated with the slogan, which blurs the sequence by which the Court built the doctrine from Baker to Gray, Wesberry and Reynolds."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/reynolds-v-sims/review/1",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How had rural Alabamians come to control the state legislature by the early 1960s?",
   "uso:answer": "Although the 1901 constitution required reapportionment after each census, the legislature had not reapportioned since the 1900 census while nearly all of the state's growth came in urban areas; by the 1960 census about a quarter of the population lived in districts that could elect a majority of each house.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:reynolds-v-sims"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/reynolds-v-sims/review/2",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why did the Court reject the argument that a state senate could be modeled on the U.S. Senate?",
   "uso:answer": "It held that the federal arrangement grew out of a unique historical compromise among sovereign states, whereas counties and other political subdivisions are subordinate instruments of the state, so the federal analogy did not apply to state legislatures.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:reynolds-v-sims"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/reynolds-v-sims/review/3",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How did Reynolds build on Baker v. Carr?",
   "uso:answer": "Baker (1962) held that apportionment challenges could be heard by federal courts; Reynolds supplied the substantive standard, requiring both houses to be apportioned so that districts were as nearly equal in population as practicable.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:reynolds-v-sims"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/reynolds-v-sims/review/4",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How did opponents of the ruling try to reverse it, and why did they fail?",
   "uso:answer": "They proposed a constitutional amendment allowing one house of a state legislature to be apportioned using geography and political subdivisions as well as population; it won a 57 to 39 majority in the Senate on August 4, 1965, but fell short of the two-thirds a constitutional amendment requires.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:reynolds-v-sims"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/reynolds-v-sims/timeline/1",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Alabama's new constitution requires legislative seats to be reapportioned by population after each federal census; no statewide reapportionment follows the 1900 census.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1901"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/reynolds-v-sims/timeline/10",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Avery v. Midland County extends the equal-population rule to elected local governments.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1968"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/reynolds-v-sims/timeline/11",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Mahan v. Howell upholds a state legislative map with a 16.4 percent total deviation, allowing more leeway than for congressional districts.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1973"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/reynolds-v-sims/timeline/12",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Evenwel v. Abbott holds that states may draw legislative districts using total population.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "2016"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/reynolds-v-sims/timeline/2",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Young Jefferson County lawyers file suit in the U.S. District Court for the Middle District of Alabama, with M. O. Sims among the plaintiffs.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYearMonth",
    "@value": "1961-08"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/reynolds-v-sims/timeline/3",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "In Baker v. Carr the Supreme Court holds that challenges to legislative apportionment can be heard by federal courts.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1962"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/reynolds-v-sims/timeline/4",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Judges Frank M. Johnson and Richard T. Rives rule for the plaintiffs, and the court orders a temporary plan for the 1962 election.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYearMonth",
    "@value": "1962-05"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/reynolds-v-sims/timeline/5",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "In Gray v. Sanders the Court strikes down Georgia's county-unit system, declaring the principle of one person, one vote.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1963"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/reynolds-v-sims/timeline/6",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Supreme Court hears oral argument in Reynolds v. Sims.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1963-11-13"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/reynolds-v-sims/timeline/7",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "In Wesberry v. Sanders the Court requires congressional districts to be as equal in population as practicable.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1964"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/reynolds-v-sims/timeline/8",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Court decides Reynolds 8 to 1 along with companion cases from New York, Maryland, Virginia, Delaware and Colorado.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1964-06-15"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/reynolds-v-sims/timeline/9",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "A proposed constitutional amendment to let one legislative house be apportioned on factors besides population wins 57 to 39 in the Senate, short of two-thirds.",
