  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/starving-time/misconception",
   "@type": "uso:Misconception",
   "uso:fact": "Colony leaders such as John Smith, Ralph Hamor and William Strachey blamed idleness, but many historians now accept Karen Ordahl Kupperman's argument that malnutrition and disease produced an apathy that looked like laziness, while a severe drought and a Powhatan siege cut the fort off from food.",
   "uso:myth": "Jamestown's colonists starved because they were lazy gentlemen who would not work.",
   "uso:why": "The idleness story came from leaders and promoters who had their own reasons to blame the settlers rather than the Virginia Company or conditions in Virginia, and it was retold as a simple moral lesson about discipline and work."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/starving-time/review/1",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why did the Powhatan siege of 1609–1610 cause starvation rather than defeat in battle?",
   "uso:answer": "Powhatan chose starvation over direct assault; his warriors killed the colony's hogs and anyone who left the fort to hunt, fish, trade or forage, so the colonists, already short of food in a drought, could not feed themselves until the siege lifted at spring planting.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:starving-time"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/starving-time/review/2",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How did the loss of the Sea Venture contribute to the Starving Time?",
   "uso:answer": "The flagship carried much of the relief fleet's supplies and the colony's new leaders, so the ships that did arrive brought about 300 hungry colonists with little food, and Jamestown was left under weak leadership as winter came.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:starving-time"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/starving-time/review/3",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "What evidence shows that survival cannibalism occurred at Jamestown, and why do some historians remain cautious?",
   "uso:answer": "Accounts by George Percy and the 1624 Ancient Planters' report describe it, and in 2013 forensic study of a teenage girl's skull showed chop and knife marks consistent with survival cannibalism; but Rachel Herrmann notes that the written accounts had authors with motives, and no document describes that particular girl.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:starving-time"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/starving-time/review/4",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why do historians now doubt that idleness explains the deaths?",
   "uso:answer": "Karen Ordahl Kupperman argued that malnutrition and diseases such as scurvy and dysentery cause apathy and loss of appetite that contemporaries mistook for laziness, and tree-ring research shows the colony faced its worst drought in 770 years.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:starving-time"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/starving-time/timeline/1",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "A hurricane scatters the Virginia Company's nine-ship supply fleet; the flagship Sea Venture, carrying the colony's new leaders, wrecks on Bermuda.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYearMonth",
    "@value": "1609-07"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/starving-time/timeline/10",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Virginia's Ancient Planters describe the famine, including cannibalism, in a report commissioned by the General Assembly.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1624"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/starving-time/timeline/11",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "A tree-ring study in Science shows Jamestown was founded during the driest seven years in 770 years (1606–1612).",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1998-04-24"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/starving-time/timeline/12",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Smithsonian, Colonial Williamsburg and Preservation Virginia announce forensic evidence of survival cannibalism on the remains of a girl called Jane.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "2013-05-01"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/starving-time/timeline/2",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The surviving ships reach Jamestown with about 300 new colonists and few supplies.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYearMonth",
    "@value": "1609-08"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/starving-time/timeline/3",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "George Percy is elected president of the council.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1609-09-10"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/starving-time/timeline/4",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "John Smith, badly hurt in a gunpowder explosion, sails for England.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYearMonth",
    "@value": "1609-10"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/starving-time/timeline/5",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Powhatan's warriors ambush a party led by John Ratcliffe and Powhatan orders a siege of the fort.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYearMonth",
    "@value": "1609-11"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/starving-time/timeline/6",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The siege lifts in early May as the Powhatans begin spring planting; about 60 colonists remain alive at the fort.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYearMonth",
    "@value": "1610-05"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/starving-time/timeline/7",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Thomas Gates and the Sea Venture survivors reach Jamestown in two boats built on Bermuda.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1610-05-24"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/starving-time/timeline/8",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Gates announces that the colonists will abandon Jamestown and sail for England.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1610-06-07"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/starving-time/timeline/9",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The departing colonists meet the fleet of the new governor, Lord De La Warr, and are ordered back to Jamestown.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1610-06-08"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/stock-market-crash-of-1929/misconception",
   "@type": "uso:Misconception",
   "uso:fact": "Only about 3 million of roughly 120 million Americans, under 2.5 percent, owned any stock in 1929, most of them in small holdings; the widespread ruin came later, as banks failed and jobs disappeared during the Depression.",
   "uso:myth": "Most Americans had their savings in the stock market, so the crash ruined ordinary families across the country at a stroke.",
   "uso:why": "The crash is remembered through the Depression that followed, so the hardship of the 1930s is often projected back onto a few days of trading that directly touched a small minority."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/stock-market-crash-of-1929/review/1",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How did buying on margin make the crash worse once prices began to fall?",
   "uso:answer": "Investors had paid only part of a share's price and borrowed the rest against the shares, an arrangement that worked only while prices kept rising; once prices fell, the borrowed positions could not be sustained, and panic selling drove prices down further.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:stock-market-crash-of-1929"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/stock-market-crash-of-1929/review/2",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why do many economists say the crash did not by itself cause the Great Depression?",
   "uso:answer": "The economy had already begun contracting in the summer of 1929, the crash's effects on spending faded within months and recovery looked likely by fall 1930, and it was later problems in the financial system, above all bank failures, that turned a recession into a depression; some, like Allan Meltzer, call the crash a symptom of the downturn rather than a cause.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:stock-market-crash-of-1929"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/stock-market-crash-of-1929/review/3",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How did the crash change federal regulation of Wall Street?",
   "uso:answer": "It led the Senate to investigate stock exchange practices; the Pecora hearings exposed questionable dealings such as Charles Mitchell's tax-avoiding stock sale and built support for the Banking Act of 1933, which separated commercial from investment banking and created deposit insurance, and the Securities Exchange Act of 1934.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:stock-market-crash-of-1929"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/stock-market-crash-of-1929/timeline/1",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Dow Jones Industrial Average stands at 63.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYearMonth",
    "@value": "1921-08"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/stock-market-crash-of-1929/timeline/10",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "By mid-November the Dow has lost almost half its value.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYearMonth",
    "@value": "1929-11"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/stock-market-crash-of-1929/timeline/11",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Senate authorizes its Banking and Currency Committee to investigate stock exchange practices.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1932-03-02"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/stock-market-crash-of-1929/timeline/12",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Dow bottoms at 41.22, about 89 percent below its 1929 peak.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1932-07-08"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/stock-market-crash-of-1929/timeline/13",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Roosevelt signs the Securities Exchange Act of 1934.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1934-06-06"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/stock-market-crash-of-1929/timeline/14",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Dow finally regains its 1929 high.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1954-11-23"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/stock-market-crash-of-1929/timeline/2",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Federal Reserve Board asks reserve banks to deny credit to member banks that lend to stock speculators.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1929-02-02"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/stock-market-crash-of-1929/timeline/3",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Board approves a rise in the New York Fed's discount rate to 6 percent.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYearMonth",
    "@value": "1929-08"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/stock-market-crash-of-1929/timeline/4",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Dow closes at its peak of 381.17.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1929-09-03"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/stock-market-crash-of-1929/timeline/5",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Black Thursday: nearly 13 million shares trade; leading New York banks buy blue-chip stocks to steady prices.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1929-10-24"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/stock-market-crash-of-1929/timeline/6",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "President Hoover tells the press that the nation's basic business is sound.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1929-10-25"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/stock-market-crash-of-1929/timeline/7",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Black Monday: the Dow falls nearly 13 percent.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1929-10-28"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/stock-market-crash-of-1929/timeline/8",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Black Tuesday: the Dow falls nearly 12 percent on record trading of over 16 million shares.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1929-10-29"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/stock-market-crash-of-1929/timeline/9",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Before trading opens, the New York Fed announces it will buy $100 million in government securities.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1929-10-30"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/supreme-court-of-the-united-states/misconception",
   "@type": "uso:Misconception",
   "uso:fact": "Article III creates \"one supreme Court\" but sets no size; the Judiciary Act of 1789 gave it six justices, Congress changed the authorized number several times, and the figure of nine dates only from an 1869 statute.",
   "uso:myth": "The Constitution requires the Supreme Court to have nine justices.",
   "uso:why": "Because nine has stood for more than 150 years, an ordinary act of Congress is easily mistaken for a constitutional rule."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/supreme-court-of-the-united-states/review/1",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why can Congress change the number of Supreme Court justices without amending the Constitution?",
   "uso:answer": "Article III creates \"one supreme Court\" but says nothing about its size, so the number is set by statute; the Judiciary Act of 1789 set six, and later laws moved it between five (never in effect) and ten before an 1869 act fixed it at nine.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:supreme-court-of-the-united-states"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/supreme-court-of-the-united-states/review/2",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How did Marbury v. Madison expand the Court's role in the system of checks and balances?",
   "uso:answer": "Chief Justice John Marshall held that part of the Judiciary Act of 1789 conflicted with the Constitution and that courts must follow the Constitution when a statute conflicts with it, the first time the Court declared an act of Congress unconstitutional and the basis of judicial review.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:supreme-court-of-the-united-states"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/supreme-court-of-the-united-states/review/3",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why did Franklin D. Roosevelt's 1937 plan to enlarge the Court fail?",
   "uso:answer": "Many saw it as a threat to judicial independence; a bipartisan Senate Judiciary Committee majority urged its emphatic rejection, the Court began upholding New Deal-era laws such as the minimum-wage law in West Coast Hotel v. Parrish, Justice Van Devanter retired, and the Senate defeated the plan on July 22, 1937.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:supreme-court-of-the-united-states"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/supreme-court-of-the-united-states/review/4",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How does most of the Court's work reach it, and how selective is it?",
   "uso:answer": "Most cases arrive as petitions for a writ of certiorari asking the Court to review a lower-court decision; four justices must agree to take a case, and of up to about ten thousand requests a year the Court accepts fewer than 2 percent.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:supreme-court-of-the-united-states"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/supreme-court-of-the-united-states/timeline/1",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Judiciary Act of 1789 organizes the Court with a chief justice and five associate justices.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1789-09-24"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/supreme-court-of-the-united-states/timeline/10",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Congress reduces the authorized seats to seven, to be reached as vacancies occur.",
   "uso:date": {
