   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/treaty-of-paris-1783/misconception",
   "@type": "uso:Misconception",
   "uso:fact": "The treaty was made between Britain and the United States alone, signed only by their commissioners, and contained no terms at all for Britain's Native allies; Britain signed away only its own claims, and each nation was left to make its own peace with the United States, which at Fort Stanwix in 1784 treated four of the Six Nations as defeated enemies. A Native confederacy north of the Ohio went on to defeat U.S. armies in 1790 and 1791.",
   "uso:myth": "In the Treaty of Paris, the Native nations east of the Mississippi gave up their lands to the United States.",
   "uso:why": "The treaty's boundary lines put the whole region between the Appalachians and the Mississippi inside the United States on paper, which makes it easy to read a European diplomatic agreement as if it settled who actually held and governed the land."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/treaty-of-paris-1783/review/1",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why did the American commissioners sign preliminary terms with Britain in November 1782 that could not take effect at once?",
   "uso:answer": "The United States was allied with France, so the preliminary articles were written to take effect only after Britain and France agreed their own peace terms; France and Britain signed their preliminaries on January 20, 1783, and the definitive treaty followed on September 3.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:treaty-of-paris-1783"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/treaty-of-paris-1783/review/2",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How did Article 7 of the treaty, which barred the British from carrying away enslaved people, end up being applied in New York?",
   "uso:answer": "Sir Guy Carleton read it as not covering Black people who had already joined the British, so they could not be returned as property; about 3,000 in New York received passes recorded in the Book of Negroes, and upwards of 9,000 people of African descent left with the British from New York and other ports.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:treaty-of-paris-1783"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/treaty-of-paris-1783/review/3",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why did the treaty leave the Haudenosaunee and other Native nations to negotiate separately with the United States?",
   "uso:answer": "The treaty was between Britain and the United States only and contained no negotiated terms for Britain's Native allies, so each nation had to make its own peace, as the Six Nations did under pressure at Fort Stanwix in 1784.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:treaty-of-paris-1783"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/treaty-of-paris-1783/review/4",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How did disputes over the treaty's debt and western-post terms help make the case for a stronger national government?",
   "uso:answer": "Virginia closed its courts to British creditors because Britain had broken the treaty, and British garrisons stayed at Detroit, Niagara and other posts inside U.S. borders; Federalists argued that only a Congress able to tax and raise an army could force Britain to leave.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:treaty-of-paris-1783"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/treaty-of-paris-1783/timeline/1",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Formal peace negotiations open in Paris between British envoys and the American commissioners Benjamin Franklin, John Adams and John Jay.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1782-09-27"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/treaty-of-paris-1783/timeline/2",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The British and American commissioners sign preliminary articles of peace, to take effect only once Britain and France agree terms.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1782-11-30"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/treaty-of-paris-1783/timeline/3",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Britain and France sign their own preliminary articles, and an armistice is declared.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1783-01-20"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/treaty-of-paris-1783/timeline/4",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "David Hartley and the three American commissioners sign the definitive treaty at the Hôtel de York in Paris; Britain's treaties with France and Spain are signed at Versailles the same day.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1783-09-03"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/treaty-of-paris-1783/timeline/5",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Confederation Congress, meeting in Annapolis, ratifies the treaty.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1784-01-14"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/treaty-of-paris-1783/timeline/6",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "With no terms for Britain's Native allies in the treaty, U.S. commissioners dictate a separate peace to the Six Nations at Fort Stanwix.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1784-10-22"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/triangle-shirtwaist-factory-fire/misconception",
   "@type": "uso:Misconception",
   "uso:fact": "Max Blanck and Isaac Harris were indicted for manslaughter but acquitted on December 27, 1911, after the jury was asked whether they knew the ninth-floor door was locked. Civil suits were settled in 1914 for $75 per life lost, and in 1913 Blanck was fined just $20 for again locking a factory door during working hours.",
   "uso:myth": "The Triangle owners were convicted and punished for the deaths.",
   "uso:why": "The fire is remembered for the sweeping reforms that followed, so it is easy to assume the law also held the owners accountable; in fact the reforms came largely because existing law had failed to."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/triangle-shirtwaist-factory-fire/review/1",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why did so many workers on the ninth floor die when most on the eighth and tenth floors escaped?",
   "uso:answer": "The ninth-floor workers were trapped by a locked stairwell door, blocked exits and a single overwhelmed elevator; the fire escape collapsed and firefighters' ladders could not reach that high, so many died inside or jumped.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:triangle-shirtwaist-factory-fire"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/triangle-shirtwaist-factory-fire/review/2",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How did the fire lead to new labor laws in New York?",
   "uso:answer": "Public outrage and lobbying led the legislature to create the Factory Investigating Commission in June 1911; its hearings and inspections of thousands of workplaces produced dozens of state laws on fire safety, sanitation, machine guarding and limits on working hours for women and children.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:triangle-shirtwaist-factory-fire"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/triangle-shirtwaist-factory-fire/review/3",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How did the fire's influence reach the national level two decades later?",
   "uso:answer": "Frances Perkins witnessed the fire and worked for the commission; as Franklin Roosevelt's Secretary of Labor she applied lessons from New York's reforms nationally, and New Deal laws of the 1930s adopted similar standards.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:triangle-shirtwaist-factory-fire"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/triangle-shirtwaist-factory-fire/review/4",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why were the owners acquitted despite testimony that the doors would not open?",
   "uso:answer": "The jury had to decide whether Blanck and Harris knew the door was locked at the time of the fire, and defense lawyer Max Steuer raised enough doubt on that point to win a not-guilty verdict.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:triangle-shirtwaist-factory-fire"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/triangle-shirtwaist-factory-fire/timeline/1",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The ten-story Asch Building is completed at 23–29 Washington Place in Greenwich Village; the Triangle Waist Company later occupies its eighth, ninth and tenth floors.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1901"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/triangle-shirtwaist-factory-fire/timeline/10",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The building, renamed the Brown Building, is designated a National Historic Landmark.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1991-07-17"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/triangle-shirtwaist-factory-fire/timeline/11",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Researcher Michael Hirsch identifies the last six unidentified victims.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "2011"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/triangle-shirtwaist-factory-fire/timeline/2",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Triangle workers join the garment strike known as the Uprising of the 20,000, which lasts about three months.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1909"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/triangle-shirtwaist-factory-fire/timeline/3",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Fire breaks out on the eighth floor near closing time; the first alarm goes out at 4:45 p.m. and 146 workers die.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1911-03-25"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/triangle-shirtwaist-factory-fire/timeline/4",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The ILGWU organizes a funeral procession; about 100,000 people march behind the hearses on Fifth Avenue.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1911-04-05"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/triangle-shirtwaist-factory-fire/timeline/5",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "A grand jury indicts owners Max Blanck and Isaac Harris for manslaughter.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1911-04-11"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/triangle-shirtwaist-factory-fire/timeline/6",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The New York legislature creates the Factory Investigating Commission.",
   "uso:date": {
