  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/world-war-ii-and-the-united-states/misconception",
   "@type": "uso:Misconception",
   "uso:fact": "The country was neutral in law but not in practice. From 1939 it sold arms to Britain and France on a cash-and-carry basis, in September 1940 it traded destroyers to Britain for bases, and from March 1941 the Lend-Lease Act let it supply Britain and other nations without immediate payment.",
   "uso:myth": "The United States stayed strictly neutral in World War II until the attack on Pearl Harbor.",
   "uso:why": "Pearl Harbor was such a sudden shock, and isolationist sentiment before it so loud, that the long, deliberate drift toward aiding the Allies is easily forgotten."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/world-war-ii-and-the-united-states/review/1",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How was the United States involved in the war before Pearl Harbor even though it was officially neutral?",
   "uso:answer": "It sold war materiel to Britain on a cash-and-carry basis from 1939, traded more than 50 destroyers to Britain for base leases in 1940, and under the March 1941 Lend-Lease Act supplied Britain and eventually more than 30 countries with about $50 billion in aid without demanding immediate payment.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:world-war-ii-and-the-united-states"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/world-war-ii-and-the-united-states/review/2",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why did the United States end up fighting Germany as well as Japan after Pearl Harbor?",
   "uso:answer": "Congress declared war only on Japan on December 8, 1941. On December 11 Germany and Italy declared war on the United States, and Congress then passed resolutions recognizing a state of war with both.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:world-war-ii-and-the-united-states"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/world-war-ii-and-the-united-states/review/3",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why did African American newspapers promote a \"Double V\" campaign during the war?",
   "uso:answer": "About a million Black Americans served, mostly in segregated units led by white officers, while discrimination persisted at home; the Pittsburgh Courier's campaign urged victory over fascism abroad and over racism at home.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:world-war-ii-and-the-united-states"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/world-war-ii-and-the-united-states/review/4",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How did the war change the American economy and workforce?",
   "uso:answer": "Federal spending soared, unemployment virtually disappeared as some 20 million workers entered the military, women took over a million administrative jobs and became a majority of aircraft-industry workers by 1943, and the bracero program brought Mexican laborers to fill farm jobs.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:world-war-ii-and-the-united-states"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/world-war-ii-and-the-united-states/timeline/1",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "War breaks out in Europe; Roosevelt declares the United States neutral in law, and a new Neutrality Act lets warring nations buy American arms only on a cash-and-carry basis.",
   "uso:date": {
